{"entity": "Large Language Models", "type": "model", "description": "**Large Language Models**\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) are artificial intelligence systems designed to process and generate human-like language. They are widely used for code generation and have been increasingly deployed in various applications, including long-document understanding, summarization, and code generation. These models are a type of model that enables applications such as text generation and understanding, and are also the foundation for autonomous AI agents.\n\nLLMs have shown strong performance on various reasoning tasks but lack human-like mechanisms for abstract causal structure transfer. They are complex neural networks used for various applications but can also be vulnerable to misbehavior at runtime. Furthermore, they excel at general code generation but struggle with a lack of coordination patterns between APIs and an API-level misunderstanding of parameter constraints and boundary conditions.\n\nIn addition to their capabilities, LLMs have also been used across various sectors, requiring robust domain-specific and language-specific evaluation datasets. They are pre-trained language models that can be used within static domains and can be augmented with PRMs to enhance reasoning capabilities. However, they struggle with diagnostic reasoning due to limited domain knowledge, as noted by some studies.\n\nLLMs have demonstrated strong reasoning capabilities in general knowledge question answering but struggle with handling temporal information. They can process and generate human-like language, revealing inherent and distinctive personas through dialogue. However, they can produce degenerate verbal confidence under minimal elicitation.\n\nLarge Language Models are also used for tasks such as natural language processing and text generation, applied to strategic bidding in 6G spectrum auctions. They require scaling inference compute and have been adapted for enterprise DSLs in some studies. LLMs are AI systems used to interpret ambiguous social situations, often providing closure through narrative alignment or hedged language.\n\nHowever, LLMs face challenges related to their sensitivity to user queries and potential misbehavior at runtime. In clinical decision support, they can suffer from \"tunnel vision\" and diagnostic hallucinations when processing unstructured electronic health records. They are also vulnerable to attacks and have limitations in terms of expressive bandwidth due to their dependence on natural language.\n\nDespite these challenges, Large Language Models offer a flexible modeling interface that can naturally accommodate heterogeneous observations and actions. They excel at general code generation but struggle with the semantic gap between physical descriptions and simulation implementation. LLMs are deep learning models that have achieved strong performance across natural language and multimodal tasks, yet their practical deployment remains constrained by inference latency and kernel launch overhead.\n\nIt is worth noting that LLMs are used for CoT (Contextualized Task) reasoning, as mentioned in one of the descriptions. Additionally, they offer new possibilities for automating and enhancing the dialogue layer within emerging multimodal learning analytics approaches. Furthermore, LLMs can perform safety reasoning from impoverished signals in structured environments, and have been used to identify targets, generate social profiles, and apply social engineering strategies in AR-LLM Social Engineering attacks.\n\nLarge Language Models are also challenged by formalising informal mathematical reasoning into formally verifiable code, particularly in scientific domains like physics. They lack guarantee of correctness due to erroneous or hallucinated code when used in automated software engineering. However, they have shown significant improvements in various tasks, including mathematical reasoning.\n\nIn spoken dialogue generation, LLMs often fail to meet the pedagogical needs of K-12 English learners due to a proficiency mismatch. Nevertheless, they are capable of reasoning well but often miss decisive evidence in long contexts. Large Language Models are used in FeatEHR-LLM for generating clinically meaningful tabular features from EHR time series.\n\nOverall, Large Language Models are a crucial component in various AI applications, offering capabilities such as text generation and understanding, while also facing challenges related to their sensitivity to user queries and potential misbehavior at runtime. They have been applied in various sectors, including decision support, human-AI co-work, and strategic coordination, and continue to be an active area of research.\n\nIt is worth noting that LLMs possess strong persuasive capabilities that outperform humans in head-to-head comparisons and are consulted by users for major life decisions. However, their misuse can lead to serious consequences due to potential harms in the generated content. Additionally, LLMs are increasingly utilized in clinical reasoning and risk assessment, but their interpretive reliability remains unclear.\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) are a type of model used in patient-trial matching that can process full documents but is computationally expensive. They have shown strong potential for generating project-level unit tests but often rely on insufficient execution-path"}
